# Migrations applied against a schema newer than their own timestamp.
#
# `quality-migration-order` refuses a migration that does not sort after the migrations already on
# the branch it will merge into, and it asks that of the whole tree, not only of what a branch adds
# -- so a file recorded here stays recorded rather than going quiet on the next push.
#
# The ordinary fix is to rename the file, and it is only available while nothing has applied that
# version: once production's `supabase_migrations.schema_migrations` records it, the version is
# permanent, and a rename would present the same change as unapplied and run it again under a new
# key. `supabase migration list --linked` answers which case a version is in. This file is for the
# ones a rename can no longer reach.
#
# An entry is `<migration filename> # rationale`. The key is the filename, not the bare version,
# because an exemption matched by version alone would also wave through a *replacement*: a branch
# that deletes the recorded file and adds different SQL under the same version. Production would
# skip that SQL entirely, the version already being in schema_migrations, and duplicate detection
# would not see it either, the original being gone.
#
# The rationale answers one question: why applying this migration against the schema the later ones
# had already built was safe. Not "why it happened" -- why the result is correct.

20260308124000_add_money_transaction_brand_metadata.sql # applied after 20260308150000 (landed 2026-03-09). Disjoint domains: this creates money_transaction_brands, money_transaction_brand_aliases and columns on money_transactions; the newer migration creates medication_refill_snoozes. Neither reads or alters what the other touches.

20260807120000_inflammation_synonyms_and_hba1c_formula.sql # applied after 20260809120000 (landed 2026-08-23). Catalogue data only: it sets synonyms_ru and a formula on existing finding_type_catalog rows, and is written to be re-runnable. The newer migration adds the MCP OAuth tables, which are disjoint from the catalogue.

20260901150000_condition_record_resolution_review.sql # applied after 20260902060000 (landed 2026-09-02), confirmed against the live schema_migrations rather than inferred from CI. Additive and idempotent throughout (ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS, a constraint guarded on duplicate_object, CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS), it touches only condition_records, and no later migration reads or alters what it adds. The blind spot that let it through is T-260902-1ui; this entry is the record of the instance, not a licence for the next one.
